Writing a list or a map held as state, on android.

Compose holds a list-typed state var as a `SnapshotStateList` behind a `val`,
so `xs = [9, 8]` came out as `xs = listOf(9, 8)`: a val cannot be reassigned,
and a `List` is not a `SnapshotStateList` either. The list's contents are
replaced instead, with the value bound first because it may read the list
being cleared -- `xs = xs.filter(…)` is the case that needs it.

A map is a read-only `Map` in a `mutableStateOf`, so `mp["b"] = 2` came out as
an index write Kotlin has no `set` operator for. The entry write rebuilds the
map, which is also the write Compose recomposes on.

deny out/kotlin/android/app/src/main/java/test/sngl/app/MainScreen.kt `xs = listOf` -- a SnapshotStateList held by a val is not reassigned
deny out/kotlin/android/app/src/main/java/test/sngl/app/MainScreen.kt `mp\["b"\] =` -- a read-only Map has no set operator

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

var xs = [1, 2, 3]
var mp map<string, int> = {"a"=1}

window {
    text(value="{xs.length()} {mp.length()}")
    button(text="go", @click {
        xs = [9, 8]
        xs = xs.filter(func(x int) => x > 8)
        mp["b"] = 2
        mp["a"] += 5
    })
}
